WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.905
AI output can be useful and wrong at the
same time.

2
00:00:04.985 --> 00:00:11.861
A hallucination is a confident statement
that is unsupported or incorrect.

3
00:00:11.941 --> 00:00:16.991
In code review, that may mean inventing a
missing check,

4
00:00:17.071 --> 00:00:23.303
naming a function that does not exist,
assuming a framework version,

5
00:00:23.383 --> 00:00:28.325
or describing a data flow that is not
actually present.

6
00:00:28.405 --> 00:00:34.422
False positives are reported issues that
are not valid in context.

7
00:00:34.502 --> 00:00:39.122
False negatives are issues the AI misses
entirely.

8
00:00:39.202 --> 00:00:44.467
Both are normal enough that critical
review is mandatory.

9
00:00:44.547 --> 00:00:50.993
Verification means checking claims against
the code, configuration,

10
00:00:51.073 --> 00:00:56.123
tests, dependency versions, deployment
assumptions,

11
00:00:56.203 --> 00:01:01.468
and runtime behavior when that information
is available.

12
00:01:01.548 --> 00:01:07.780
If the AI says access control is missing,
find the route, middleware,

13
00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:12.909
service call, policy check, and object
ownership rule.

14
00:01:12.989 --> 00:01:16.213
If it says input validation is weak,

15
00:01:16.293 --> 00:01:23.706
identify exactly which input reaches which
operation without an adequate check.

16
00:01:23.786 --> 00:01:27.117
If it recommends a library function,

17
00:01:27.197 --> 00:01:34.288
confirm that advice is appropriate for the
language, framework, and version.

18
00:01:34.368 --> 00:01:39.633
Distinguish exploitability from
theoretical weakness.

19
00:01:39.713 --> 00:01:46.267
A pattern may be risky in general but not
reachable in the scoped context.

20
00:01:46.347 --> 00:01:53.331
Another issue may look small but become
serious because of a trust boundary or

21
00:01:53.411 --> 00:01:55.022
privilege level.

22
00:01:55.102 --> 00:01:58.111
The reviewer owns that judgment.

23
00:01:58.191 --> 00:02:03.670
Use AI to generate questions, identify
evidence to collect,

24
00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:11.379
and draft explanations, but do not let
confident language substitute for analysis.

25
00:02:11.459 --> 00:02:16.831
The right question is not whether the
model sounds certain.

26
00:02:16.911 --> 00:02:22.820
The right question is whether the evidence
supports the finding.
